The test creates `inner_optimizer = Adam(...)` and wraps it in `LossScaleOptimizer(inner_optimizer=inner_optimizer, ...)`. On the finite-gradients call, `LossScaleOptimizer._stateful_handle_finite_grads()` unscales the gradients and invokes `self.inner_optimizer.apply(...)`, so execution continues in the wrapped `Adam` optimizer rather than in the wrapper. In `BaseOptimizer.apply()`, `_backend_apply_gradients()` calls `_backend_update_step()`, and that method loops over `(grad, var)` pairs and dispatches with `self.update_step(grad, var, learning_rate)`. Because `self` is the `Adam` instance, the concrete implementation reached is `Adam.update_step` defined in `keras/src/optimizers/adam.py`.

